Logic

Cyclic Sort is used when the array contains numbers from 1 to n with no duplicates.

For each index:

Find the correct index of the current element.
If the current element is not at its correct index, swap it with the element at its correct index.
Otherwise, move to the next index.
Continue until all elements are in their correct positions.

Example:

3 5 2 1 4

3 belongs at index 2
2 5 3 1 4

2 belongs at index 1
5 2 3 1 4

5 belongs at index 4
4 2 3 1 5

4 belongs at index 3
1 2 3 4 5

Code :

import java.util.*;

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] arr = {3, 5, 2, 1, 4};
        cyclicSort(arr);
        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(arr));
    }

    static void cyclicSort(int[] arr) {
        int i = 0;

        while (i < arr.length) {
            int correct = arr[i] - 1;

            if (arr[i] != arr[correct]) {
                int temp = arr[i];
                arr[i] = arr[correct];
                arr[correct] = temp;
            } else {
                i++;
            }
        }
    }
}

Time Complexity 

Best case: O(n)
Average case: O(n)
Worst case: O(n)

Space Complexity
O(1)
